  "teaId": "canaanite-mother",
  "text": "A foreign mother argues theology with Jesus in public and he tells her she won.\n\nMatthew fifteen. Jesus goes into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon, and a woman of Canaan comes out after him, crying, \"Have mercy on me, O Lord, thou Son of David. My daughter is grievously vexed with a devil.\"\n\nAnd the text says something that stops you cold. \"But he answered her not a word.\"\n\nSilence.\n\nThen the disciples get involved, and not helpfully. \"Send her away, for she crieth after us.\" They're not moved, they're annoyed.\n\nThen he says he was sent to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.\n\nShe doesn't leave. She comes and worships him and says three words. \"Lord, help me.\"\n\nAnd then he gives her the hard line. \"It is not meet to take the children's bread, and to cast it to dogs.\"\n\nNow people argue endlessly about his tone there. The text does not give us tone, so hold your theory loosely. What isn't in dispute is her comeback.\n\n\"Truth, Lord. Yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters' table.\"\n\nShe takes his own metaphor, accepts the terms, and turns it.\n\nAnd he says, \"O woman, great is thy faith. Be it unto thee even as thou wilt.\"\n\nHealed that hour.",
